course: dictionary
lesson: 5678
title: Loan: borrowed money and books
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A loan is something you borrow, and you must give it back. It can be money from a bank, or a book from the library. «The book is on loan» means someone has borrowed it for a while.
explain: When a bank gives you money as a loan, you pay it back bit by bit, and you usually pay extra money called interest. «Some lenders charge huge interest, so £200 borrowed can grow to £400 owed.» The extra money is the cost of borrowing.
explain: Loan can also be a verb, which means to lend. Check the words around it: if someone is doing the lending, it is a verb, and if it is a thing that was borrowed, it is a noun. Swap in your meaning to see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A loan is money or something lent that must be paid or given back.
fact: «A mortgage is a huge loan from a bank to buy a home», usually paid back over about 25 years.
fact: Interest is the extra money you pay for borrowing.
fact: «On loan» means borrowed for a while, as with library books.
fact: To loan means to lend, so a bank can loan money to a family.
exercise: «A mortgage is a huge loan from a bank to buy a home.» What is a loan? | Money that is given away and never returned | A prize for winning a game | A type of house | *Money or something borrowed that must be paid or given back
exercise: Libraries loan books. What does loan mean in this sentence? | To throw away a book | To buy a book forever | To read a book aloud | *To lend, so you can borrow the book
exercise: «Some lenders charge huge interest, so £200 borrowed can grow to £400 owed.» What does interest mean here? | *The extra money you pay for borrowing | The number of pages in a loan | A kind of bank building | The price of the book you bought
test: «Sam took out a loan to buy a bike.» What does take out a loan mean? | *Borrowed money that must be paid back | Lost his savings on a trip | Spent all his savings on sweets | Gave money to a friend
test: Why does a loan cost more than the amount borrowed? | Because the bike is heavy | Because banks like to count coins | Because loans are always free | *Because interest is added to the amount owed
exam: Borrow $100 at 10% interest. How much do you pay back in total? | $100 | *$110 | $90 | $1,000
